      "difficulty": 2,
      "keyType": "inference",
      "passage": "The European Ombudsman investigates complaints about maladministration in the institutions, bodies, offices and agencies of the European Union. Maladministration is understood broadly: it covers not only unlawful acts but also unfairness, unjustified delays, refusal to provide information and failure to reply. The office was created by the Maastricht Treaty, and the first Ombudsman took up duties in 1995; the post-holder is elected by the European Parliament after each European election, for a renewable term matching the Parliament's.\n\nAny citizen or resident of the EU, as well as any company, association or other body with a registered office in a Member State, may lodge a complaint, normally within two years of becoming aware of the problem. The complainant must first have tried to resolve the matter with the institution concerned. Complaints about national, regional or local authorities fall outside the remit, even when those authorities are implementing EU law, and the Court of Justice acting in its judicial role cannot be investigated either.\n\nThe Ombudsman cannot issue binding decisions. Instead, the office may propose a solution, make recommendations and, if an institution does not respond satisfactorily, submit a special report to the Parliament. In practice, much of its influence comes from the publicity that such findings attract.",
