      "difficulty": 3,
      "keyType": "inference",
      "passage": "Before adopting the euro, an EU Member State must meet the convergence criteria set out in the Treaties. Its inflation rate must not exceed by more than 1.5 percentage points that of the three best-performing Member States in terms of price stability, and its long-term interest rates must not be more than 2 percentage points above those of the same three countries. Its government deficit must not exceed 3% of GDP, and its government debt must not exceed 60% of GDP unless the ratio is diminishing sufficiently and approaching that reference value. Finally, the country must have taken part in the Exchange Rate Mechanism (ERM II) for at least two years without severe tensions, and its national legislation, including the statute of its central bank, must be compatible with the Treaties.\n\nDenmark negotiated an opt-out and is not obliged to adopt the euro. The other Member States outside the euro area are legally committed to joining once they meet the criteria, but because participation in ERM II is voluntary, a country can in practice delay adoption by staying out of the mechanism. Croatia, which met all the criteria, became the twentieth member of the euro area in January 2023.",
