      "id": "v01-05",
      "topic": "Science",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "keyType": "combination",
      "passage": "Tardigrades, sometimes called water bears, are microscopic animals rarely longer than half a millimetre. More than 1,000 species have been described, living in habitats as varied as deep-sea sediments, Antarctic lakes and the damp moss on city walls. They are famous for surviving conditions that would kill almost any other animal, including extreme cold, intense radiation and prolonged drying out.\n\nTheir main survival strategy is a state known as cryptobiosis. As its surroundings dry out, a tardigrade curls into a compact 'tun', loses almost all of its body water and reduces its metabolism to an almost undetectable level. In this state some individuals have revived after years, and in a 2007 experiment some even survived exposure to the vacuum of space. Researchers have also identified proteins that appear to protect tardigrade cells and DNA from damage while they are dried out.\n\nStrictly speaking, however, tardigrades are not extremophiles. They do not thrive in extreme environments; they merely endure them until normal conditions return, and active tardigrades are no more resistant than many other small invertebrates. Most species spend their lives in ordinary, damp habitats.",
